"Structured category for an incident timeline event."
enum AIOpsIncidentTimelineIccCategory {
  "Affected services on the incident (the service(s) believed to be experiencing impact). Due to last-occurrence dedup, only the latest cumulative value survives — which contains all services added across the incident's lifetime."
  AFFECTED_SERVICES_ADDED
  "Initial assignee added to the incident. Due to first-occurrence dedup, only the earliest assignee event survives — later modifications are dropped as churn."
  ASSIGNEE_ADDED
  "A stakeholder comms update was posted (e.g. StatusPage - External or Internal URL set)."
  COMMS_POSTED
  "The incident issue was created."
  INCIDENT_CREATED
  "An AIOps RCA investigation completed."
  INVESTIGATION_COMPLETED
  "An AIOps RCA investigation was triggered."
  INVESTIGATION_STARTED
  "A linked alert was created. Timestamp comes from the alert's own createdAt field."
  LINKED_ALERT_CREATED
  "The Major-incident flag was declared (toggled ON). Due to first-occurrence dedup, only the earliest declaration survives — subsequent toggle-off/back-on cycles are dropped, since the moment of first declaration is the diagnostic anchor."
  MAJOR_INCIDENT_DECLARED
  "Incident priority was set or changed (e.g. P3 → P1)."
  PRIORITY_CHANGED
  "The incident was resolved or closed."
  RESOLVED
  "Initial responders added to the incident. Due to first-occurrence dedup, only the earliest responder event survives — later modifications are dropped as churn."
  RESPONDERS_ADDED
  "Incident severity was set or changed (e.g. SEV-3 → SEV-1)."
  SEVERITY_CHANGED
  "An SLA was breached."
  SLA_BREACHED
  "An SLA cycle completed without breach."
  SLA_MET
  "An SLA cycle started."
  SLA_STARTED
  "Incident status changed (e.g. Open → In Progress)."
  STATUS_CHANGED
}

"""
Importance tier for an incident timeline event.
Used by the server-side trim algorithm and by consumers that want to filter/sort
without re-implementing category priority rules.
"""
enum AIOpsIncidentTimelineIccImportance {
  "Headline events — should almost always be shown (creation, resolution, severity/priority set, SLA breach, investigation lifecycle)."
  CRITICAL
  "Significant operational signals — included whenever budget allows (status transitions, SLA start/met)."
  HIGH
  "Background churn — usually displaced under tight budgets (responders, other field changes)."
  LOW
  "Useful context — included if not displaced by higher tiers (assignee, comms posted)."
  MEDIUM
}

